     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "AF rate control (typically to < 100/min at rest): beta-blockers and non-DHPs (verapamil, diltiazem) are first-line; IV for acute rapid rates, and orally for long-term rate control. Don't give AV node blockers in WPW with an accessory pathway (wide QRS): risk of VF."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "IV verapamil:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " 5–10 mg (0.075–0.15 mg/kg) over at least 2 min; repeat 10 mg after 30 min if the response isn't adequate; give under continuous ECG and BP monitoring."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Also contraindicated:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " verapamil in severe LV dysfunction and in AF/flutter with an accessory pathway (WPW); non-DHPs in sick sinus syndrome without a working pacemaker."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Interactions:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " limit simvastatin to 20 mg/day with amlodipine; with diltiazem, limit simvastatin to 10 mg and diltiazem to 240 mg/day."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Verapamil can raise digoxin levels 50–75% in the first week; monitor digoxin levels when starting, adjusting or stopping diltiazem."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Avoid grapefruit juice with nifedipine; it may also raise verapamil levels. Avoid CYP3A4 inducers with nifedipine (eg, phenytoin) and diltiazem (eg, rifampin)."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Overdose:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " DHPs cause marked hypotension, possibly with reflex tachycardia; non-DHPs cause bradycardia, hypotension and heart block. Treat with IV calcium, fluids and pressors; atropine or pacing for bradycardia/heart block. Observe a verapamil overdose for at least 48 h (especially ER)."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
